  <h2>Five Postures in Sequence: The Ultimate Goal</h2>
  <p>When your stamina and endurance have improved, you can try practicing all five postures in succession, holding each for 5 minutes:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Order</th><th>Posture</th><th>Duration</th><th>Energy Direction</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Wu Chi</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Rooting — like a tree establishing roots</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Holding the Balloon</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Cultivating — embracing the chi field</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Holding in Front</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Guiding — pressing forward on floating balloons</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Splitting Water</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Expanding — parting to both sides</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Holding at Dantian</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>Gathering — collecting energy to the Dantian</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <h4>Complete Practice Flow</h4>
    <p>Every practice session must include three components: <strong>warm-up → Zhan Zhuang → cool-down</strong>.
    Skipping the cool-down causes the energy generated during practice to disperse without being gathered.</p>
  </div>

